---
import BlogPostLayout from "../../layouts/BlogPostLayout.astro";
import PhotoPlaceholder from "../../components/PhotoPlaceholder.astro";
import { getBlogPost } from "../../data/blogPosts.js";

const post = getBlogPost("cost-of-a-baby");
---
<BlogPostLayout
  title={post.title}
  description={post.description}
  eyebrow="Money"
  dek={post.dek}
  date={post.date}
  readTime={post.readTime}
  ctaHeading="Want the exact line-item numbers?"
  ctaBody="The free version is the framework. The Dudela Prep Kit has the actual dollar figures we used, month by month, plus what we'd cut first if money got tight."
  ctaHref="/kit"
  ctaLabel="Get the Prep Kit"
>
  <p>
    Every dad hears the same warning before the baby arrives: "it's expensive." Nobody follows
    that up with an actual number. So you're left doing the math yourself, at 11pm, on your
    phone, guessing — which is a bad way to make a budget and a worse way to walk into
    fatherhood.
  </p>

  <p>
    Here's the honest version: there isn't one number, because it depends on where you live, what
    insurance you have, and whether you're paying for childcare or not. But there are real
    categories, and most of them are more predictable than you'd think once you break them down.
  </p>

  <PhotoPlaceholder label="Dad holding baby, going through receipts" ratio="16/9" />

  <h2>The one-time hit before baby even arrives</h2>
  <p>
    Car seat, crib or bassinet, stroller, a chunk of clothes you'll immediately outgrow, and — if
    you're not doing a hospital birth on a plan that covers it — a chunk of medical costs up
    front. This is the number that scares people, but it's also the most controllable one: almost
    all of it can be bought used, borrowed, or covered by a registry. This is the one category
    where pride costs you real money. Nobody's judging the secondhand car seat as long as it's
    within its expiration date and wasn't in a wreck.
  </p>

  <h2>The recurring costs nobody mentions until you're in them</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Diapers and wipes.</strong> This is a real monthly line item for two-plus years, not a
    rounding error. It adds up fastest in the newborn stage, when you're changing constantly.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Feeding.</strong> Formula, if you're using it, is one of the more expensive recurring
    costs in year one. Breastfeeding is "free" in the sense that no product is purchased, but it's
    not free in terms of what it demands from your partner — worth factoring into how you're
    splitting the load, not just the budget.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Childcare.</strong> If both of you are working and daycare is part of the plan, this
    is very likely the single biggest number on this whole list — bigger than diapers, formula,
    and gear combined, depending on your area. It's also the cost most worth researching and
    getting on a waitlist for early, before you actually need it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Healthcare.</strong> Well-baby visits, vaccines, the occasional sick visit — even with
    decent insurance, copays add up in a way that catches people off guard in year one
    specifically, since that's when visits are most frequent.
  </p>

  <div class="pull-quote">
    The couples who feel calm about money aren't the ones who make the most — they're the ones
    who actually looked at the number instead of avoiding it.
  </div>

  <h2>The budget moves that actually make a difference</h2>
  <p>
    You don't need a finance degree for this part. A few moves consistently save more than any
    single gadget on a registry:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Get on a daycare waitlist the moment you know childcare is part of the plan — this is a time cost, not a money one, and it's the easiest to put off and regret.</li>
    <li>Buy secondhand for anything the baby will outgrow in under six months — clothes, the early-stage car seat, the bassinet.</li>
    <li>Check what your insurance actually covers for well-baby visits before you're surprised by a bill.</li>
    <li>Set a number for what you're comfortable spending on "gear you don't strictly need" before you're standing in the store making that call on the fly.</li>
  </ul>

  <PhotoPlaceholder label="Nursery / gear laid out, secondhand and new mixed together" ratio="4/5" />

  <h2>The number that actually matters</h2>
  <p>
    Not the total. The number that matters is whatever amount lets you and your partner stop
    flinching every time a baby expense comes up. That's different for every family, and it's the
    thing the Prep Kit walks through — the real figures we used, and what we'd have cut first if
    we'd had to.
  </p>
</BlogPostLayout>
